< Those two rounds of defection, at the opening and again after every slip, are the point, and they are why I called the machine the handshake: two of a kind go through the same small ritual before they trust each other. In a world of plain reciprocators, who repay like with like, players who simply always cooperate do at least as well. They drift in unnoticed, and then defectors feed on them. Against this machine, a player who always cooperates never gets through the opening (the second game above), so that door is shut. A player who always defects does get through the opening, but gains nothing by it. If one player defects and the other cooperates, the defector gets 5 points and the cooperator gets nothing; when both cooperate each gets 3, and when both defect each gets 1. Against this machine the player who always defects collects the 5 only every third round (the third game above) and falls behind. And because slips keep happening, the machine's way of punishing a defection is in constant use and does not wither.
---
